CAT = "excptIntake"; SEG = "INT"
# Domain 4A (13 of 100): tasks 4A.1 to 4A.12, knowledge K.74 to K.91.
Q = [
("A new prescription has no directions for use. What does the technician do?",
 "Call the prescriber for them", ["Use the most common directions", "Ask the patient how to take it", "Print \"use as directed\""],
 "Directions for use are a required part of a prescription. Missing information is obtained from the prescriber and documented, not guessed."),
("A dentist prescribes a blood pressure medicine for a patient's hypertension. Why is it questioned?",
 "It is outside dental practice", ["Dentists cannot prescribe at all", "It lacks a DEA number", "Dentists cannot send e-scripts"],
 "Prescriptive authority follows the provider's scope. A dentist prescribes for dental treatment, such as an antibiotic or pain reliever after a procedure."),
("Which item belongs in a patient profile and must be checked on every new prescription?",
 "Drug allergies", ["Favorite pharmacy staff", "The patient's employer", "The patient's preferred bag size"],
 "Profiles hold demographics, allergies, health conditions, current medicines including OTCs, and payer information. Allergies are checked each time a drug is entered."),
("How many digits are in a prescriber's National Provider Identifier (NPI)?",
 "10", ["12", "11", "8"],
 "45 CFR 162.406: the NPI is a 10-position number with a check digit in the tenth position. It carries no information about the provider."),
("A prescription is phoned in by the prescriber's office. What must the receiving person do with it?",
 "Write it down at once", ["Wait for a faxed copy", "Wait for the e-script to arrive", "Ask the patient to confirm it"],
 "An oral prescription is written down or entered promptly with all required information. State law sets who may receive it."),
("What does pc mean on a prescription?",
 "After meals", ["Before meals", "With a full glass of water", "Every other day"],
 "pc comes from post cibum, after meals. ac, ante cibum, means before meals."),
("A sig reads 1 tab po qid. How often does the patient take it?",
 "Four times a day", ["Every other day", "Three times a day", "Every 4 hours"],
 "qid means four times a day. tid is three times a day and q4h is every 4 hours."),
("A sig reads 1 supp pr q6h prn nausea. How is it used?",
 "Rectally every 6 hours if needed", ["By mouth every 6 hours", "Under the tongue every 6 hours as needed", "Vaginally every 6 hours"],
 "supp is a suppository, pr means by rectum and prn means as needed."),
("A sig reads ii gtt AD bid. Where are the drops placed?",
 "In the right ear", ["In the left ear canal", "In both eyes", "In the right eye"],
 "AD refers to the right ear, AS the left ear and AU both ears. OD, OS and OU refer to the eyes, which is why ear and eye abbreviations are easy to confuse."),
("A prescriber leaves product selection open, and the patient asks for the brand. Which DAW code is used?",
 "DAW 2", ["DAW 0", "DAW 1", "DAW 5"],
 "DAW 2 means substitution was allowed but the patient requested the brand. DAW 0 means no product selection indicated and DAW 1 means the prescriber did not allow substitution."),
("A generic is not in stock, so the brand is dispensed even though substitution was allowed. Which DAW code fits?",
 "DAW 4", ["DAW 2", "DAW 3", "DAW 1"],
 "DAW 4 means substitution allowed, generic drug not in stock. DAW 3 means the pharmacist selected the product."),
("Which number on a prescription card routes the claim to the right processor?",
 "The BIN", ["The NPI", "The NDC", "The DEA number"],
 "The BIN (bank identification number) directs the claim to the processor, and the PCN routes it further. The member ID identifies the patient."),
("A claim rejects with the message refill too soon. What does this usually mean?",
 "Records show supply left", ["The patient's coverage ended", "The drug is not on the formulary", "The NDC is invalid"],
 "A refill-too-soon reject means the plan's records show enough medicine left from the last fill. The pharmacist decides whether an override is warranted."),
("A claim rejects for prior authorization. Who must act to get it approved?",
 "The prescriber's office", ["The technician, alone", "The patient's employer", "The drug manufacturer"],
 "Prior authorization is a coverage review the prescriber's office completes with the plan. The technician can tell the prescriber and the patient about the reject."),
("A patient has two insurance plans. What does coordination of benefits do?",
 "Bills primary first, then secondary", ["Bills both plans the full amount", "Bills only whichever plan costs less", "Bills the patient, then both plans"],
 "Coordination of benefits makes sure the primary payer pays first and the secondary payer covers some or all of the rest, so the claim is not paid twice."),
("What is a formulary?",
 "A list of covered drugs", ["A list of drug prices", "A book of compounding recipes", "A list of recalled drugs"],
 "A formulary lists the drugs a plan covers, often placed in tiers. Non-formulary drugs may need prior authorization or cost more."),
("In a tiered copay plan, which drugs usually have the lowest copay?",
 "Preferred generics", ["Non-preferred brands", "Specialty drugs", "Non-formulary drugs"],
 "Lower tiers, usually generics, carry the lowest copay. Specialty and non-preferred brand tiers cost the patient more."),
("A Medicare patient brings a prescription for blood glucose test strips. Which part of Medicare usually covers them?",
 "Part B", ["Part A", "Part C only", "Part D"],
 "Medicare Part B covers blood glucose test strips and blood sugar monitors as durable medical equipment and supplies."),
("What is a copay?",
 "A set amount paid per fill", ["The yearly plan premium", "The pharmacy's cost for the drug", "A rebate from the maker"],
 "A copay is a fixed amount the patient pays for each covered prescription. Coinsurance is a percentage of the cost instead."),
("A refill request comes in for a prescription with no refills left. What does the technician do?",
 "Ask the prescriber for a refill", ["Dispense a 3-day supply", "Tell the patient to see the doctor", "Refill it once as a courtesy"],
 "Technicians often send refill authorization requests by fax, phone or e-prescribing, so the prescriber can approve a new prescription."),
("While entering a prescription, the system shows a drug-allergy alert. What does the technician do?",
 "Bring it to the pharmacist", ["Override it if the reaction was mild", "Ask the patient if it still applies", "Note it on the label and continue"],
 "Clinical alerts such as allergies and interactions are reviewed by the pharmacist. Technicians do not override them."),
("Which item must a prescription for a non-controlled drug include?",
 "The prescriber's name", ["The patient's diagnosis code", "The patient's insurance ID", "The prescriber's DEA number"],
 "Required elements include the patient's name, date, drug, strength, quantity, directions and the prescriber's name and signature. A DEA number is needed only for controlled substances."),
("Which drug use review alert means a new prescription is in the same class as a drug the patient already takes?",
 "Therapeutic duplication", ["Drug-disease contraindication", "Incorrect duration", "Drug-allergy interaction"],
 "Therapeutic duplication is two or more drugs from the same class whose combined dose may put the patient at risk. It is one of the prospective DUR screens."),
("Which error does sending prescriptions electronically (e-prescribing) mainly reduce?",
 "Misread handwriting", ["Wrong copay amounts", "Late deliveries", "Short counts"],
 "E-prescriptions arrive as typed data, so errors from illegible handwriting are avoided. The technician still checks every field."),
]
